{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Research Translation for Learning Design", "title": "Publication-Ready Artifact Packaging", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting publication-ready artifact packaging to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design", "ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 6, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "07", "section": "06", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/07/05"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_calibration"]}, "content": "# Publication-Ready Artifact Packaging\n\n![Publication-Ready Artifact Packaging textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/07-06-publication-ready-artifact-packaging.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA design artifact that only its author can use is not finished; it is a private draft. Publication-ready packaging is the discipline of preparing an artifact so a competent stranger can identify the canonical version, understand its intent, reuse it legally, and reproduce its use, all without contacting you. This is the reproducibility standard that scholarly and open-source communities have converged on: a clear README, an unambiguous canonical file, recorded provenance for every reused asset, an explicit license, and a changelog. The folder of \"FINAL\" and \"FINAL (1)\" files is the anti-pattern this section exists to eliminate.\n\nFor a learning designer, packaging is where evidence of your reasoning either survives or evaporates. If the decision log, the asset sources, and the AI-assistance notes are not written down at packaging time, they are usually lost. Treating packaging as a design step, not a clerical afterthought, is what makes your work citable, auditable, and reusable.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Identify the reuse test: could a competent stranger find the canonical artifact and reproduce its use from the package alone?\n- Build a package with a README (intent and decision log), a single dated canonical file, per-asset provenance, an explicit license, and a changelog.\n- Record asset provenance (source and license) rather than silently reusing images.\n- Decide what to include and what to summarize (e.g., disclose AI assistance in the README rather than dumping a full chat log).\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teammate says the storyboard is \"done\" and shows you the folder: `storyboard_final_v2_FINAL.pdf`, `storyboard_final_v2_FINAL (1).pdf`, an `images/` directory, and a `notes.txt`. The work may be excellent, but the package fails immediately: a stranger cannot tell which PDF is canonical, the reused images carry no license, and nothing states what the artifact is for or how to use it. \"Done\" here means the author can still open it; it does not mean anyone else can. This section turns that folder into a package that passes the reuse test.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nPublication-ready packaging is organized around one operational standard, the reuse test, supported by five components:\n\n| Component | What it guarantees | Failure if missing |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| README | Intent, decision log, how to view, AI-assist and verification note | Reuser cannot tell what the artifact is for |\n| Canonical file (single, dated) | One unambiguous source of truth | \"Which FINAL is the real one?\" |\n| Per-asset provenance | Source and license for every reused image or text | Legal and ethical reuse risk |\n| LICENSE | Explicit terms for reuse | Reuser does not know what is permitted |\n| CHANGELOG | Version history and what changed | No way to track or cite a specific version |\n\nThe components serve the reuse test: a competent stranger should be able to open the README, find the canonical file, confirm they may reuse the assets, and rebuild the work. Packaging also includes a judgment about *what not to include*: the goal is the provenance a reuser needs, not every artifact you produced. A full AI chat transcript, for instance, is noise; the README's statement of which sections the AI drafted and how you verified them is the signal.\n\nThe course anchors apply even at the packaging stage. **Mayer's principles** shape the README so it communicates efficiently (a coherent, signposted document, not a wall of text), because a README that overloads the reader defeats its purpose. **Cognitive load theory** argues for structure that lowers the reuser's extraneous effort: a manifest and clear naming reduce the search cost of finding the canonical file. **UDL learner variability** reminds you that \"competent stranger\" includes reusers with different tools and access needs, so the package should not assume your exact software environment.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"README\",\"definition\":\"Guarantees the reuser can tell what the artifact is for, how to view it, and how AI assistance was verified; absent, the reuser cannot tell the intent.\"},{\"term\":\"Single dated canonical file\",\"definition\":\"Guarantees one unambiguous source of truth; absent, the reuser asks which FINAL is the real one.\"},{\"term\":\"Per-asset provenance\",\"definition\":\"Guarantees every reused image or text records a source and license; absent, there is legal and ethical reuse risk.\"},{\"term\":\"LICENSE\",\"definition\":\"Guarantees explicit reuse terms; absent, the reuser does not know what is permitted.\"},{\"term\":\"CHANGELOG\",\"definition\":\"Guarantees a version history; absent, there is no way to track or cite a specific version.\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A folder holds a beautiful storyboard.pdf, an images/ directory, and nothing else. Why does it still fail the reuse test?\" explanation=\"Polish is a property of the artifact; reusability is a property of the package. With no README, no license, and no recorded provenance for the images, a competent stranger cannot tell what it is for or whether they may legally reuse it.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It passes; a polished PDF is publication-ready.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Polish does not supply intent, a license, or asset provenance, so a stranger cannot reuse it.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It fails only because the PDF is not dated.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nTrace one artifact package from \"looks done\" to genuinely reusable by an outsider.\n\n**Before (package contents, first pass):**\n\n```\nstoryboard_final_v2_FINAL.pdf\nstoryboard_final_v2_FINAL (1).pdf\nimages/\nnotes.txt\n```\n\n1. Two near-identical \"final\" files, with no manifest to say which is canonical.\n2. Reused images in `images/` with no recorded source or license.\n3. No statement of intent and no instructions for viewing or rebuilding.\n\n**After (publication-ready package):**\n\n```\nREADME.md            # intent, decision log, how to view, AI-assist + verification note\nstoryboard.pdf       # canonical artifact (single, dated)\nassets/              # each image with source + license recorded in README\nLICENSE              # CC BY 4.0\nCHANGELOG.md         # version history\n```\n\nThe revised package passes the reuse test: the canonical artifact is unambiguous, every reused asset carries provenance, and the README lets a competent stranger rebuild the work.\n\nOne rejected inclusion: the full AI chat log. I left it out because a reuser does not need the transcript; instead the README states which sections the AI drafted and how I verified them, which is the provenance that actually supports reuse and accountability.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nPackage the artifact, then run the reuse test as a thought experiment with a stranger:\n\n1. **Canonical check:** Could the stranger identify the single authoritative file without guessing? If two files compete for \"final,\" fix the naming and the manifest.\n2. **Provenance check:** Does every reused asset record a source and a license? If an image's origin is unknown, you cannot ship it.\n3. **Rebuild check:** Do the README instructions let the stranger view or reproduce the artifact in their own environment, without your help?\n4. **Inclusion check:** Does the package contain the provenance a reuser needs and exclude noise (raw logs, dead drafts) that obscures it?\n\nA package that fails any check is not \"almost done\"; it is a private draft wearing a \"final\" label.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Run the reuse test as a stranger\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You inherit a folder: storyboard_final_v2_FINAL.pdf, storyboard_final_v2_FINAL (1).pdf, images/, notes.txt. As a competent stranger, where do you begin the reuse test?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Open the prettier of the two PDFs and start reusing it.\",\"next\":\"guess\",\"feedback\":\"Guessing the canonical file is exactly the failure the test exists to catch.\"},{\"label\":\"Run the canonical check: can I identify the single authoritative file without guessing?\",\"next\":\"canonical\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Ambiguous canonical state is the first blocker.\"}]},\"guess\":{\"prompt\":\"You picked the wrong PDF and rebuilt from a stale draft. Back up: what should you have established first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A single, dated canonical file named in a manifest.\",\"next\":\"canonical\",\"feedback\":\"Yes, that removes the guesswork.\"}]},\"canonical\":{\"prompt\":\"You name storyboard.pdf as the dated canonical file. Next you check the reused images in images/. They have no recorded source or license. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Ship them anyway; they look generic.\",\"next\":\"unlicensed\",\"feedback\":\"Unknown origin means you cannot legally or ethically ship them.\"},{\"label\":\"Run the provenance check: record source and license for each asset, or remove it.\",\"next\":\"provenance\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. No provenance, no reuse.\"}]},\"unlicensed\":{\"prompt\":\"A reuser is later sued over an unlicensed image. What was the missing check?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Per-asset provenance: source and license recorded for every asset.\",\"next\":\"provenance\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly.\"}]},\"provenance\":{\"prompt\":\"Assets now carry provenance in the README. A teammate wants to include the full AI chat log for transparency. Do you?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes; include everything to be transparent.\",\"next\":\"dump\",\"feedback\":\"Dumping the transcript buries the canonical file and relevant provenance under noise.\"},{\"label\":\"No; the README states which sections AI drafted and how you verified them.\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Good packaging is curation toward reuse, not exhaustive archival.\"}]},\"dump\":{\"prompt\":\"The package now hides the canonical file under raw logs. What is the right inclusion rule?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Include the provenance a reuser needs; summarize AI use in the README.\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"Yes, signal over noise.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"Package: README with intent and AI-verification note, single dated storyboard.pdf, assets/ with provenance, LICENSE, CHANGELOG. A competent stranger can find the canonical file, confirm reuse rights, and rebuild it without you.\",\"feedback\":\"The package now passes the reuse test.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading equates a polished artifact with a publication-ready one. A beautiful PDF in an unlicensed, unlabeled folder still fails the reuse test, because polish is a property of the artifact and reusability is a property of the package around it. A second misreading treats \"include everything\" as transparency. Dumping every draft and every chat log does not aid a reuser; it buries the canonical file and the relevant provenance under noise. Good packaging is curation toward reuse, not exhaustive archival.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the standard to a dataset-plus-dashboard artifact. The first pass is a folder with `data.csv`, `dashboard.html`, and `final_charts.xlsx`, no README. Walk the checks. Canonical: name the authoritative dashboard file and date it, and state in the README that `data.csv` is the source for `dashboard.html`. Provenance: record where `data.csv` came from, how it was collected, and any de-identification applied, plus the license under which it may be reused. Rebuild: write the steps to open the dashboard and regenerate the charts, naming the tool versions so a stranger on different software can adapt. Inclusion: drop the stale `final_charts.xlsx` if the dashboard supersedes it, or mark it explicitly as deprecated in the changelog. The result is a package a reviewer could cite by version and a peer could rebuild, which is the whole point.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead your own package as a stranger who has never met you and cannot ask you anything. For each file, ask \"would I know what this is and whether I may use it?\" and for the folder as a whole, ask \"could I find the one real version and rebuild it?\" A strong reader notices the gaps that authors are blind to: the unlicensed image, the undated duplicate, the assumed software, the missing decision log. The discipline is to package for the reuser you will never meet, not for the author who already knows everything.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove packaging into a regulated or archival context, such as depositing an artifact in an institutional repository that requires persistent identifiers and long-term access. The reuse test tightens: the canonical file now needs a stable identifier, the license must permit the repository's terms, and the README must remain intelligible years after the software that made the artifact is gone. The same five components carry over, but the \"competent stranger\" becomes a future reuser with none of your context and possibly none of your tools. Designing the package to survive that gap is the proof that you understand packaging as future-proofing reuse, not as tidying a folder.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\", \"ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/07-06-publication-ready-artifact-packaging-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"07.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 07.06. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Publication-Ready Artifact Packaging, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging", "stem": "What makes a learning-design artifact package publication-ready rather than just submitted?", "options": ["It looks polished and has a nice cover image.", "Another designer could reuse or rebuild it from the package alone: versioned files, a README of intent and decisions, attribution, and a license.", "It is large and includes every working file you ever made.", "It is zipped and emailed on time."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Publication readiness is about reproducibility and reuse by someone else, not appearance or size. The package must carry enough provenance to stand alone.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_polish_equals_ready", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design", "ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging", "stem": "Your package uses a stock image and a colleague's rubric. What must the package include to be ethically reusable?", "options": ["Nothing; if it is in your folder you can publish it.", "Source and license for the stock image and credit plus permission for the colleague's rubric, recorded in the package.", "A note that says all rights reserved on everything.", "Only your own name on the cover."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Provenance and licensing for reused materials are part of a publishable package. Without them, the artifact cannot be lawfully or ethically reused.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_no_provenance", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design", "ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "You used an AI tool to generate part of the package contents. What belongs in the publication-ready package about that use?", "options": ["Nothing; AI assistance never needs to be recorded.", "A note stating which components the AI helped produce and how you verified them, so a reuser can judge provenance.", "A claim that you wrote everything yourself.", "The full chat log pasted in without context."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Reproducibility includes honest provenance about AI contributions and verification, scoped to what a reuser needs, not a raw transcript.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_no_provenance", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design", "ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging", "stem": "A reviewer downloads your package and cannot tell which file is the final storyboard or how to run the prototype. What does this reveal about the package?", "options": ["The reviewer should try harder; the files are all there.", "The package fails the reuse test: it lacks a manifest and a README that name the canonical files and the steps to run them.", "Nothing important; final files are obvious by date.", "The package needs more files added."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "If a competent outsider cannot identify the canonical artifact and reproduce its use, the package is not publication-ready regardless of how complete it feels to the author.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_polish_equals_ready", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design", "ail606_supplement_publication_ready_artifact_packaging", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "0ace2d1ad34b47656c5f1f92c3dcd0d966cc0d098e18a331dc9458d4301bf060", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_polish_equals_ready", "pattern": "polish_mistaken_for_reproducibility", "description": "Equating a visually polished or on-time submission with a publication-ready package that another designer could rebuild and reuse", "trigger": "learner judges readiness by appearance, size, or deadline rather than by whether an outsider could reproduce the artifact's use", "feedback": "Test the package for reuse: can a competent stranger find the canonical files and reproduce the work from the package alone?", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_no_provenance", "pattern": "missing_provenance_and_license", "description": "Publishing reused materials, or AI-assisted components, without source, license, attribution, or verification notes", "trigger": "learner includes stock media, a colleague's rubric, or AI output with no provenance recorded", "feedback": "Record source, license, attribution, and how AI-assisted parts were verified. Provenance is part of the publishable package.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_7_6_publication_ready_artifact_packaging_no_manifest", "pattern": "no_manifest_or_